                  <provenance type="#m"><orgName role="fmo" key="org_496">Sheen, Surrey.
                        Charterhouse of Jesus of Bethlehem</orgName><p>Cambridge, Emmanuel Coll. 35,
                        Trinity Coll. 345, and the Hilton in Philadelphia contain notes in the hand
                        of James Grenehalgh (professed 1495, see H. E. Allen). Grenehalgh also
                        annotated Bodleian, Douce 262 (see under London, Charterhouse), BL, Royal 5
                        A.v (see under Coventry, Charterhouse) and his monogram is in BL, Add. 24461
                        and 37790 and probably Harley 2373 (see under Mount Grace).</p><p>The
                        character of the notes in this copy suggest that Greenhalgh owned
                     it.</p></provenance>
                  <provenance type="#rejected"><orgName role="fmo" key="org_537">Syon, Middlesex,
                        Bridgettine abbey of St Saviour, St Mary the Virgin, and St
                        Bridget</orgName>
                     <p>A Syon attribution was rejected in the second edition of Medieval Libraries
                        of Great Britain (1964), which did not change in the Supplement (1987). The
                        corresponding Medieval Libraries of Great Britain index card records the
                        following information, which was deemed unsatisfactory for an attribution:
                           <q>See Miss H. E. Allen, <title>Writings ascribed to Richard
                              Rolle</title>, p. 47, 217, etc.</q> The same index card (i.e. for the
                        rejected Syon attribution) also originally included the following note:
                           <q>Previously belonged to John Newton, treasurer or York Minster
                           (d.1414).</q> However, Ker later added several question marks beside
                        this, writing: <q><q>Tabula huius libri additur per mag. Joh. Neuton</q> is
                           the only mention of J. N. in James's catalogue.</q></p></provenance>
                  <provenance type="MLGB3_laterOwners">The names Michael and Anna Purefey and Thomas
                     Fawnte are scribbled on fols. 191r, 192r.</provenance>
                  <provenance type="MLGB3_laterOwners">Geo. Davenport 1652.</provenance>
               </history>
